    {
      "content": "# CI, audits, and deployment\n\nThis repository uses a pnpm workspace with a Next.js app at `apps/www`. GitHub Actions runs lint, type checks, unit/component test commands, Playwright E2E and axe accessibility checks, and Lighthouse CI.\n\n## Local checks\n\n```sh\npnpm install\npnpm exec playwright install chromium\npnpm run lint\npnpm run typecheck\npnpm run test:unit\npnpm run test:component\npnpm run test:e2e\npnpm run audit:lighthouse\n```\n\nThe Lighthouse command expects the production app to be listening on `http://127.0.0.1:3000`. To run it locally, build and start the app in one terminal, then run `pnpm run audit:lighthouse` in another.\n\n## Lockfile\n\nA pnpm lockfile is not currently present in the repository. Generate and commit it with `pnpm install` before relying on reproducible installs. Then change CI and Docker installs from `pnpm install --no-frozen-lockfile` to `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and enable pnpm caching in Actions.\n\n## Deployment architecture\n\n- Pushes to `develop` run CI, build an immutable GHCR image tagged with the commit SHA, then deploy it to the GitHub `staging` environment.\n- Pushes to `main` run the same CI gates, then deploy to the GitHub `production` environment.\n- Configure required reviewers on `production` in Settings → Environments if production releases should require approval.\n- Deploy workflows use SSH to run `docker compose pull` and `docker compose up -d` in the configured server deployment directory. They do not provision servers, DNS, TLS, firewall rules, Compose files, or application environment files.\n\n## GitHub environment configuration\n\nCreate two GitHub Environments named exactly `staging` and `production` in Settings → Environments. Configure each environment independently with the following **secrets**:\n\n| Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `DEPLOY_HOST` | SSH server hostname or IP |\n| `DEPLOY_USER` | Restricted SSH deployment account |\n| `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` | Private key for that account; do not commit it |\n| `DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS` | Verified SSH host key entry, obtained and checked out-of-band |\n\nAdd this **environment variable** to each environment:\n\n| Variable | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `DEPLOY_PATH` | Absolute server directory containing `compose.yaml` |\n\nOn each server, place `compose.example.yaml` as `compose.yaml` in `DEPLOY_PATH`, create the required `.env` file, install Docker Engine and the Compose plugin, and grant the deployment account only the permissions it needs. The server must be able to pull the repository's GHCR package. For a private package, authenticate Docker on the server with a narrowly scoped read-only package credential; never put that credential in the repository or deployment workflow. Alternatively, configure the GHCR package as public.\n\nThe workflow verifies SSH using `DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS`; do not disable host-key checking. The GHCR image is tagged with the commit SHA rather than a mutable `latest` tag so deployments are traceable and can be rolled back by redeploying a known image tag.\n\n## Audits\n\nAxe and Lighthouse automate some accessibility checks only. They do not certify WCAG conformance and cannot replace keyboard testing, screen-reader testing, or manual review. Fix reported issues in the UI and rerun the audits; do not suppress violations without documenting a justified exception.\n",
